---
import { getImage } from 'astro:assets';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import scallopPattern from '../assets/counselling-room/scallop-pattern.png';

// Same tiled background as the "Where we meet" (CounsellingRoom) section,
// for one consistent pattern across the page.
const scallopPatternTile = await getImage({ src: scallopPattern, width: 560 });

const sections = [
	{
		title: 'You are not a problem.',
		body: [
			'First, let’s be clear: you are not a problem. I am not here to fix you, because you are not broken. For a long time, autism, ADHD and dyslexia were seen as deficits — a neurodiverse person was seen as “less” of a person.',
			'Now we know that isn’t the case. Research into neurodiversity shows it isn’t the neurodiverse person who is the cause of the challenges they face. I am here to help you find a way to live a good life, as a neurodiverse person.',
		],
	},
	{
		title: 'Where is the disability and disadvantage?',
		body: [
			'Disability and disadvantage arise when we are a square peg being forced to fit into a round hole. As neurodiverse people, we are disabled by the interaction between us and a system, person or environment that isn’t built for everyone.',
			'We are disabled by non-inclusive, non-universal places and systems that exclude and disadvantage by their design. We find these non-inclusive structures in modern-day work, economic, education and medical systems, and more — frequently designed for a specific brain, culture and class of people.',
		],
	},
	{
		title: 'More than our neurodiversity?',
		body: [
			'We don’t just bring our neurodiversity into the world — we also bring the harm from a lifetime of stigma and struggle. We bring our family histories, other co-occurring conditions like anxiety or trauma, learned behaviours that have helped us survive, and financial, medical and economic circumstances.',
			'All of this intertwines with our neurodiversity and our lives, and affects how we behave. In counselling, we begin to untangle these — to heal from hurt, explore our identities, and change how we see or interact with the world, in order to live more fully as our neurodiverse selves.',
		],
	},
	{
		title: 'A neurodiverse core identity',
		body: [
			'As a neuro-affirming counsellor, I focus on living more fully, not on becoming normal.',
			'We explore what parts of our identity are core to our neurodiverse or autistic self. These parts don’t change, and they form a foundation of who we are.',
			'Other parts of us might have developed in response to trying to survive, but now do us more harm. These are parts that can change, without changing our core identity.',
		],
	},
	{
		title: 'My neuro-affirming principles',
		body: ['My neuro-affirming principles focus on five areas: identity, marginalisation, community, connection and living.'],
		list: [
			{
				name: 'Living',
				desc: 'Covers things like masking, earning a living and dealing with the challenges of life. It also covers how we live with our feelings, emotions, body and health, and safety.',
			},
			{
				name: 'Community',
				desc: 'Covers our tribe, the people around us, their welcomingness, spirituality and our social networks.',
			},
			{
				name: 'Connection',
				desc: 'Covers how we connect with others: our relationships, our friends, and how we impact other people.',
			},
			{
				name: 'Marginalisation',
				desc: 'Covers how we might have experienced stigma and disadvantage.',
			},
			{
				name: 'Identity',
				desc: 'Covers how we see ourselves, the roles we hold in life, the confusing existential questions, how we deal with the fluidity of life and meaning, and spirituality.',
			},
		],
	},
	{
		title: 'How neurodiversity changes therapy?',
		body: [
			'The most obvious change is making sure the counselling space is designed to help you regulate. This includes lighting, neutral and calming scents, textures and, of course, a variety of fidget toys for regulating. (You’ll definitely spot me using the fidget toys too.)',
			'The counselling approaches also change. Below you’ll find a brief description of a few neurodiversity-specific therapy frameworks I draw on, each offering a range of approaches beyond standard talk therapy.',
			'Brief, timed therapy programs: for many, having a clear start and end to therapy, with a structured pathway, can be incredibly useful. This is why you’ll also find a few pre-built therapy programs you can choose as a pathway.',
		],
	},
].map((section) => ({ ...section, slug: section.title.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-').replace(/(^-|-$)/g, '') }));
---

<Layout
	title="My Neuroaffirming Approach — Ben Maclaren"
	description="How Ben Maclaren, a counsellor in Canberra, ACT, works with neurodivergent clients."
>
	<article class="neuro-page">
		<div class="neuro-page-bg" style={`background-image: url(${scallopPatternTile.src})`}></div>
		<div class="wrap">
			<a class="back-link" href="/">← Home</a>
			<span class="eyebrow">Neuro-affirming</span>
			<h1>My neuroaffirming approach</h1>

			<nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
				{
					sections.map((section, i) => (
						<>
							<a class="toc-link" href={`#${section.slug}`}>
								{section.title}
							</a>
							{i < sections.length - 1 && (
								<span class="toc-arrow" aria-hidden="true">
									→
								</span>
							)}
						</>
					))
				}
			</nav>

			<div class="neuro-page-body">
				{
					sections.map((section) => (
						<section class="neuro-page-section" id={section.slug}>
							<h2>{section.title}</h2>
							{section.body.map((paragraph) => (
								<p>{paragraph}</p>
							))}
							{section.list && (
								<ol class="principles-list">
									{section.list.map((item) => (
										<li>
											<strong>{item.name}</strong> — {item.desc}
										</li>
									))}
								</ol>
							)}
						</section>
					))
				}
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>
</Layout>

<style>
	.neuro-page {
		position: relative;
		padding: var(--space-20) 0 var(--space-25);
		overflow: hidden;
	}

	.neuro-page-bg {
		position: absolute;
		inset: 0;
		z-index: 0;
		opacity: 0.06;
		background-repeat: repeat;
		background-size: 560px auto;
	}

	.neuro-page .wrap {
		position: relative;
		z-index: 1;
	}

	.back-link {
		display: inline-block;
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-size: var(--fs-caption);
		letter-spacing: var(--ls-label);
		text-transform: uppercase;
		text-decoration: none;
		color: var(--color-text-secondary);
		margin-bottom: var(--space-8);
	}

	.back-link:hover {
		color: var(--color-accent);
	}

	.eyebrow {
		margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
		display: block;
	}

	h1 {
		font-size: var(--fs-display);
		line-height: var(--lh-article-title);
		font-weight: var(--fw-medium);
		max-width: 640px;
		margin-bottom: var(--space-13);
	}

	.toc {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		align-items: center;
		gap: var(--space-2-5);
		margin-bottom: var(--space-13);
		padding-bottom: var(--space-8);
		border-bottom: var(--border-hairline) solid var(--color-border);
	}

	.toc-link {
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-size: var(--fs-caption);
		letter-spacing: var(--ls-tight-label);
		text-decoration: none;
		color: var(--color-text-secondary);
		white-space: nowrap;
	}

	.toc-link:hover {
		color: var(--color-accent);
	}

	.toc-arrow {
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
		font-size: var(--fs-caption);
	}

	.neuro-page-body {
		max-width: 640px;
	}

	.neuro-page-section {
		margin-bottom: var(--space-11);
	}

	.neuro-page-section:last-child {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}

	.neuro-page-section h2 {
		font-size: var(--fs-card-title);
		font-weight: var(--fw-medium);
		margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
	}

	.neuro-page-section p {
		font-size: var(--fs-prose-body);
		color: var(--color-text-secondary);
		margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
	}

	.neuro-page-section p:last-child {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}

	.principles-list {
		margin: var(--space-4) 0 0;
		padding-left: var(--space-5);
	}

	.principles-list li {
		font-size: var(--fs-prose-body);
		color: var(--color-text-secondary);
		margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
	}

	.principles-list li:last-child {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}

	.principles-list strong {
		color: var(--color-text-primary);
		font-weight: var(--fw-medium);
	}
</style>
